</p/><p><strong>Summary: </strong>
A season of splendor fading yet magical.
</p>
        <p>Autumn is that favorite pair of jeans, worn to the point of comfort and
familiarity.  Yet those jeans will soon develop an irreparable tear, and be
relegated to recycling.  All of those leaves, once verdant green, begin in fits,
their transition to radiance, then brittle remnants of a season passed.  The
night air, begins testing its bite—a gentle nibble which will become the fangs
of winter, drawing warmth as a vampire at the neck.</p>
<p>Yet, let us not pass so quickly to winter, for this harvest time is one of
darkening days, and remembrance of the moon’s splendor, now given more time to
shine.  When we can gather around a campfire to share our myth-making and have
more than a few precious hours before the coming dawn.  Autumn is life in the
balance; holding visible memories of what was and what will be.</p>
<p>Last night, with the moon just past full, I stepped outside, and saw close to
shore a trailing water wake, catching moonlight, streams of light vanishing,
appearing, and dancing.  I swear I saw magic revealed.</p>

